# Line endings are decided HERE, not by whichever git happens to do the checkout.
#
# Two git installations sit on the provisioning server's PATH and disagree:
# cygwin git 2.51.0 has core.autocrlf unset, Windows git 2.54.0 has it true from
# its own system gitconfig. With no `text` rule, the tool that performed the clone
# decided the working tree's line endings -- so two trees at the SAME commit built
# payloads differing in 113 files, and `git status` reported both clean because
# the stat cache short-circuits the content comparison (#216).
#
# Attributes override core.autocrlf, which is the whole point of putting it here.
# This line is FIRST so the `-text` rules below win for the artifacts that must
# stay byte-exact; the last matching rule takes precedence.
* text=auto eol=lf

server/providers/*/assets/*.zip filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
server/providers/*/assets/*.exe filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
server/providers/*/assets/*.tgz filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
server/providers/*/assets/*.msi filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
server/providers/*/assets/*.whl filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
server/providers/*/assets/*.vsix filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
server/providers/*/assets/**/*.cab filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# The bootstrap USB/ISO payload. A separate line because the pattern list above is
# per-directory: client/assets/ matched nothing, so npcap's committed pointer was
# never smudged and every clone got the 132-byte pointer text instead of the
# installer -- silently, since `git lfs pull` exits 0 on a path it does not filter.
# A new asset directory needs its own rule here. See #101.
client/assets/*.exe filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# .iso is a generated build artifact -- prefer .gitignore. The LFS rule is a
# safety net in case one is staged elsewhere by accident.
*.iso filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.msi filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.whl filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Shell scripts must check out with LF even on Windows. The provisioning server is
# a Windows host whose shell scripts run under CYGWIN bash, and cygwin bash cannot
# execute a CRLF script: it reports `set: pipefail: invalid option name`, then
# `$'\r': command not found` on every line, then a syntax error on the first loop.
#
# Caught 2026-09-17 registering the vendor-store jobs. It was invisible until then
# because the scripts had only ever been tested after `scp` from a Mac, which
# preserves LF -- the first run from a real `git` checkout on the build host was
# the first CRLF one.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Vendor artifacts that must reach a unit byte for byte. Driver INFs and catalogs
# are consumed by Windows setup and certificates are signed blobs; none of them
# may be rewritten by a line-ending filter, whatever git guesses about them.
*.inf  -text
*.sys  -text
*.cat  -text
*.cer  -text
